1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A domino has a string on top and a string on the bottom. That is all an instance is made of.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
You are given a finite set of dominoes, and you may use each one as many times as you like.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
A match is an ordering of dominoes whose top strings, read in order, equal its bottom strings, read in order.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The problem is not to find a match. It is to decide, for an arbitrary instance, whether one exists.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Emil Post posed exactly this in 1946, and called it the correspondence decision problem.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Here is one instance: a over ab, b over aa, aa over a, ab over b.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Place domino one: top reads a, bottom reads ab. The bottom is one letter ahead.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Add domino two: top ab, bottom abaa. Add domino three: top abaa, bottom abaaa.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Add domino three again, and the two rows both read abaaaa.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Nothing was needed beyond the four dominoes: the match is the ordering 1, 2, 3, 3.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
At every step of a match, one row is a prefix of the other. That is the invariant.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The unmatched tail is called the overhang, and its length is the difference of the two row lengths.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
A branch survives only if the new overhang stays compatible. This is the only pruning there is.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
On this match the overhang is b, then aa, then a, then nothing.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Every prefix of the model's match was checked, and all four satisfy the invariant.

16
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
A search grows level by level: every sequence of one domino, then two, then three.

17
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Unpruned, the first three levels hold 4, 16 and 64 sequences: 84 in all.

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
With the prefix test the same three levels hold 2, 2 and 4 survivors, and twelve nodes are examined in total.

19
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
The match sits at level four, and the model confirms no match exists at levels one, two or three.

20
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,000
This run terminated because a match exists. When none exists, the same run never stops.

21
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Here is an instance with no match: a over ab, b over bb, ba over b.

22
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Every surviving branch has the bottom longer than the top, by one more letter at each level.

23
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Two branches survive at every depth after the first, forever. The frontier never empties.

24
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
So a bounded search can prove a yes and can never prove a no.

25
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
There is one cheap no: if every top begins with a and every bottom with b, no match can start.

26
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Special cases are decidable. The general problem is not, and that is a theorem, not an observation.

27
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
MPCP is the same problem with one extra rule: the match must start with the first domino.

28
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:50,000
This MPCP instance has the match 1, 3: a over aa, then aa over a, both rows reading aaa.

29
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,000
To turn it into a PCP instance, interleave a new symbol between every pair of letters.

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
The first domino is padded on both sides, so it is the only one whose two rows start with the same symbol.

31
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
One extra domino adds the missing star at the end, and the match is complete.

32
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The padded match is 1, 4, 5 and reads star a star a star a star at on both rows.

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
The reduction starts from a Turing machine and its input, and builds dominoes out of the machine's own rules.

34
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
One domino starts the history, one copies a tape symbol, and one pair per transition moves the head.

35
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Two more kinds of domino let the accepting state eat the rest of the tape and finish.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
So a match is exactly an accepting computation history, written twice.

37
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
If PCP were decidable, the halting problem would be, and it is not. So PCP is undecidable.

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
The theorem needs at least two letters. With one letter the problem is decidable, and the next scenes show both sides.

39
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Give each domino its own marker letter, and build two languages, one from the tops and one from the bottoms.

40
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Each language is context-free: one nonterminal, one production per domino.

41
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
A word in both languages has two derivations, and the markers force the same index sequence in both.

42
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
On this instance the match 1, 2, 3, 3 gives one word with two derivations.

43
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
A sequence that is not a match gives two different words, so it produces no ambiguity.

44
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
So deciding ambiguity would decide PCP. Ambiguity of a context-free grammar is undecidable.

45
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,000
The same construction settles two more questions about context-free grammars.

46
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Intersection emptiness is undecidable for two context-free languages.

47
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:40,000
But intersection with a regular language is decidable, and so is everything about regular languages.

48
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Equivalence of two context-free grammars is undecidable; equivalence of two regular expressions is decidable.

49
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Every one of these results is a PCP reduction wearing different clothes.

50
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Now the counterexample to the theorem's hypothesis: an alphabet with one letter.

51
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Each domino has a difference: top length minus bottom length. A match needs differences that sum to zero.

52
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Three cases decide it. A zero difference wins at once; all differences the same sign loses at once.

53
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Otherwise one domino is positive and one is negative, and a fixed number of copies of each cancels exactly.

54
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
The model runs this decider on three instances and agrees with a brute-force search on all three.

55
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
So the undecidability theorem is about two letters, and the encoding into two letters is why that is enough.

56
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
First slip: 'the search found no match, so there is none'.

57
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Second slip: 'undecidable' taken to mean 'this instance has no solution'.

58
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Third slip: confusing checking a given match with deciding whether one exists.

59
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Fourth slip: reaching for Rice's theorem, which is about properties of languages, not about dominoes.

60
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Every one of the four is a sentence that is true in one setting and false in the one being asked about.

61
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:57,500
An instance is a finite set of pairs. A match is an ordering whose two rows read the same string.

62
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:03,000
One row is always a prefix of the other. That is the only pruning, and it makes the search a tree.

63
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,500
Over two letters or more the problem is undecidable, and every CFL decision result follows from it.

64
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:12,000
Over one letter it is decidable in three cases, and that is the counterexample the hypothesis needs.

